{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Hurry — three se'ahs of meal, fine flour.</b> In the chapter HaPo'alim (Bava Metzia 87a) the Talmud raises the question: it is written ״קֶמַח״ and it is written ״סֹלֶת.״ Rabbi Yitzḥak said: from here we learn that a woman's eye is stingy toward guests more than a man's. Rashi explained: he said ״סֹלֶת״ and she said ״קֶמַח.״ But according to this reversal there is no inference at all, for if he said ״קֶמַח,״ how could she be so presumptuous and so dominant as to take more than what her husband commanded her — does not everything belong to him? But through a slight diminution in what she takes out it is possible, for she would in a sense flatter her husband by doing so. Even though the language of the verse suggests that Abraham said ״קֶמַח סֹלֶת,״ nevertheless he said ״קֶמַח״ for the kneading of the cooks, and ״סֹלֶת״ for the cakes. And I have heard people say that ״קֶמַח״ is a general term inclusive of both fine and inferior grades, and Abraham let slip from his mouth incidentally the word ״קֶמַח,״ and his intention was to say: 'Take from what you have, the finest.' But then he reasoned in his heart: perhaps she will take from the inferior grades, for Abraham knew about her that her eye is stingy toward guests. Therefore he hurried and specified his words and said: ״סֹלֶת״ is what I desire. Or: at first he said ״קֶמַח״ for the kneading, and he was afraid lest she take ordinary meal also for the cakes; therefore he specified ״סֹלֶת.״ And because her eye was stingy, therefore Sarah is not mentioned in connection with this entire preparation, for she did nothing at all. Therefore it is said ״שׁוֹב אָשׁוּב אֵלֶיךָ״ (Genesis 18:10) — 'I will return to you' — and not 'to her,' for her eye is stingy toward guests. Also with regard to the son it says ״שׁוֹב אָשׁוּב אֵלֶיךָ,״ for his merit alone caused her to bear a son, in the merit of hospitality — as we find in the Shunammite woman: in the merit of ״נַעֲשֶׂה נָּא עֲלִיַּת קִיר קְטַנָּה״ and so forth (II Kings 4:10), and she would bring in guests; therefore it was said to her ״כָּעֵת חַיָּה אַתְּ חֹבֶקֶת בֵּן״ (II Kings 4:16). Scripture attributed the matter to her and not to her husband, for she was complete in hospitality and he was not — the opposite of what is said here, ״שׁוֹב אָשׁוּב אֵלֶיךָ.״ And likewise with Lot it says ״וּמַצּוֹת אָפָה״ (Genesis 19:3) — implying he and not his wife; therefore she became a pillar of salt, for she was not complete in the trait of righteousness, which is likened to salt, after the manner of ״מֶלַח מָמוֹן חָסֵר״ (Ketubot 66b)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The word רַשָּׁאָה is vocalized in some witnesses as רְשָׁאֵי ('permitted') and in others as here; translated following the given text's reading as 'presumptuous/dominant.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:47.613786979Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}